window.DDIA = window.DDIA || {chapters:[], viz:[], system:null};
window.DDIA.chapters.push({
  n: 12, title: "Stream Processing",
  worksheet: "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GAGuVaZvsqp4kuQjCQu5k5UKVsbvKIzoqC3RTtT8m3A/edit",
  why: "Chapter 11 processed a complete, bounded input with nobody waiting. This chapter removes the \"complete\": the input never ends and the output has to be close to current. It's the chapter behind every \"keep the search index fresh,\" \"fan out to followers,\" \"update the cache when the row changes,\" and \"notify the user.\" Two ideas carry it: a log is a durable, replayable, ordered stream, and derived data stays correct when it's built from the change stream of the source of truth instead of by dual-writing. \"Exactly-once\" is the trap: it's a property you construct downstream with idempotence or an atomic commit, never a flag on the broker. The last chapter of the book; after it the lane becomes mock designs. The <a href=\"#/viz/change-stream\">Change Stream Map</a> draws the whole thing. The round below wires the messaging system's change stream end to end: who publishes, who consumes, and what each consumer does when it is replayed.",
  jargon: [
    ["Event", "An immutable record that something happened, with a timestamp. Produced once, consumed by many."],
    ["Stream", "An unbounded sequence of events processed incrementally as they arrive, instead of a bounded file processed all at once."],
    ["Queue-style broker", "SQS, RabbitMQ: a message goes to one consumer, is acked, is deleted. Load-balances tasks and forgets history. Durable, but a second reader gets nothing and replay doesn't exist."],
    ["Log-based broker", "Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis: an append-only, partitioned file on disk. Reading deletes nothing; consumers keep their own offset; retention by time or compaction. A buffer that doesn't forget."],
    ["Topic", "The named log you publish to and subscribe to. Under the name, a set of partitions. Our <code>conversation-events</code> and <code>participant-changes</code>."],
    ["Partition", "One ordered sub-log of a topic, chosen by hashing the key. Total order within; no order across. Same word, same tradeoff as chapter 7; a partition holds millions of keys interleaved because partitions are heavy and keys are many."],
    ["Offset", "A message's position in its partition, assigned at append. Names the message and records where a consumer is. Unrelated across partitions; not stable across a repartition."],
    ["Consumer group", "A set of consumer instances sharing a topic's partitions, each partition read by exactly one member. Parallelism capped by partition count; a rebalance pauses the group."],
    ["Roles, not machines", "Producer writes to the log (our owner, via its relay). Broker stores and serves it. Consumer reads forward from an offset and derives something. Owner is the one we invented: the lease-holding process that decides. One machine can hold several roles."],
    ["Log compaction", "Keep only the latest value per key; delete needs a tombstone kept for a grace period. Bounded by distinct keys. Rebuilds current state per key; destroys history, so never for the message log."],
    ["Tombstone", "A record meaning \"this key was deleted,\" since an append-only structure can't remove in place. Kafka: key with null value. Cassandra: a deletion timestamp. LSM: a delete marker in the newest segment. A system primitive, not an application convention."],
    ["Dual write", "The app writes the database and then the cache/index/queue as two steps. Fails on a crash between them (silent permanent gap) and on a race between two writers (permanent wrong order), with no failure needed."],
    ["Change data capture", "A connector reads the database's own replication log, decodes row changes, and publishes them as an ordered event stream so other systems can follow the database. The write-ahead log is the thing; CDC is the tap. Not replica catch-up; that's chapter 6."],
    ["Replication slot", "How the database tracks a CDC reader's position and keeps its log until that reader advances. One connector, one slot; a connector down for a week fills the primary's disk."],
    ["Outbox", "The app writes the business row and an event row in one database transaction; a relay publishes the event. Kills the dual write; events carry intent. Production shape: CDC on the outbox table."],
    ["Event sourcing", "The application writes events as the source of truth; state is a derived view. History is the product (our message log is one). Can't be compacted; needs snapshots for replay speed and a derived view for reads."],
    ["Event time vs processing time", "When it happened vs when the pipeline saw it. Event time for anything about the world; processing time only for anything about the pipeline. Test: does the answer change if you restart the consumer?"],
    ["Window", "Tumbling (fixed, no overlap), hopping (fixed, overlapping), sliding (every event's trailing interval), session (gap-based)."],
    ["Watermark", "The processor's declaration that no event earlier than T is expected, so windows can close. A guess with one dial: tight means fast and more stragglers; loose means late and fewer. An idle input freezes it."],
    ["The three joins", "Stream–stream within a time window (searches × clicks); stream–table against a local copy fed by the table's changelog (events × participants); table–table as a materialized view that updates when either side changes (the inbox copy)."],
    ["Stream–table duality", "A table is a fold over its changelog (start empty, apply each change in order); a changelog is the stream of changes to a table. Each derives from the other; a compacted topic is where they meet."],
    ["Checkpointed state", "Flink/Kafka Streams keep operator state locally and snapshot all state plus input offsets together; recovery is restore, rewind, replay. Exactly-once inside the job for free; slow restarts with big state."],
    ["Idempotent consumer", "Applying the same event twice has the same effect as once. Naturally (set, upsert, delete-by-key) or made so (a seen-before check on event identity, which is the construction of idempotence, not a cheat around it)."],
    ["Version by log position", "Every derived row carries the offset or seq of the last event applied; apply only if newer. Makes replays, retries, and batch backfills commutative. Last-writer-wins with a log position as the clock."],
    ["Exactly-once (effectively once)", "Delivery is at-least-once; the <i>effect</i> appears once. Three constructions: idempotent effect, atomic commit of output plus offset, or recompute from the log instead of processing deliveries. Not a broker feature."],
    ["Backpressure and lag", "Offset lag (log end minus committed) and time lag (now minus last processed event time). A log absorbs a slow consumer on disk; a queue fills memory; a direct pipe blocks the sender."],
    ["Lambda vs kappa", "Lambda: batch for correctness plus stream for freshness, merged at query time. Kappa: stream only, rebuild by replaying the log through the same code. Either way: online serves, stream patches, batch rebuilds and checks."]
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: "round", title: "The round: streams in the messaging system", intro: "The interviewer has the messaging design on the board: owner processes sharded by conversation, Cassandra for messages, Postgres for participants, an inbox copy, search, push. Now they ask how the derived parts stay current.", items: [
      { q: "\"Every time a message lands you update the inbox, search, push and analytics. How?\"",
        a: "<p>Not by writing five stores from the send path. <b>One writer of truth, everything else derived from its change stream.</b> The owner publishes each message as an event on a log-based broker; five consumer groups (inbox copy, search, notifications, cross-region copy, analytics) each read the whole stream at their own pace and derive their store. A log, not a queue, because five readers need every event, each conversation needs order, and every derived store needs replay to be rebuilt.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Why not just write the row, then publish an event?\"", a: "<p>The dual write. A crash between the two leaves a committed row no consumer will ever hear about, silently and permanently. With no crash at all, two servers can commit in one order and publish in the other, so every consumer applies the wrong order. Put the event inside the one write (outbox) or read it out of the commit log (CDC).</p>" }
        ],
        line: "one writer of truth, a log in the middle, and every derived store is a consumer group that can be replayed." },
      { q: "\"Where does the change stream originate, who publishes it, and what's the partition key?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Origin: the owner tier.</b> The owner already has every message in total order, deduplicated by the client's idempotency key, fenced by its epoch, acked by the Cassandra quorum; the stream's hardest properties are things it already established. <b>Publisher:</b> the owner, via an outbox row written in the same Cassandra batch as the message (same partition key, atomic), and a relay that publishes to the topic. For Postgres tables (participants, conversations): CDC via logical decoding, one connector. <b>Key: <code>conv_id</code></b>, the owner's shard key, so per-conversation order on the topic is seq order and one consumer instance handles a conversation at a time. Every event carries <code>(conv_id, seq)</code> as a free idempotency key.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Why not tap Cassandra's write-ahead log with CDC like you do for Postgres?\"", a: "<p>Cassandra's log is per node and unordered across replicas; a tap gives three partial, differently ordered views of one write. The owner already serializes the conversation, so it publishes. CDC is for the store the app writes directly, which here is Postgres.</p>" },
          { q: "\"Analytics wants its own feed from the primary.\"", a: "<p>No second replication slot. Each slot pins the primary's log until its reader advances, so the slowest reader holds the disk hostage. One connector taps the database; analytics subscribes at the broker like the other four groups.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "the owner already serializes each conversation, so it publishes; the topic is keyed by conversation; everything else is a consumer group." },
      { q: "\"Owner, producer, broker, consumer. Are those Kafka words or roles?\"",
        a: "<p>Roles, not machines; general, with Kafka names for some. Every owner is a producer; not every producer is an owner (the owner decides in the write path, publishing is a side duty). If the owner served consumers directly it would have to buffer per consumer, retain for the slowest, survive its own death, fan out to sixty connections and absorb backpressure on the send path: it would have become a durable, replicated, offset-tracking log server, a broker, as its second job next to the one that must be fast. The broker is a buffer that doesn't forget: rate decoupling plus durability plus replication is the whole product.</p>",
        line: "the owner decides; the broker remembers; the consumer derives; collapse any two and the survivor has to do both jobs." },
      { q: "\"You partition by conversation ID. So one partition per conversation?\"",
        a: "<p>No. <code>hash(conv_id) mod 64</code> puts all of conversation A in one partition, in order, but 64 partitions (each is files plus a replication stream plus a leader) against billions of conversations means each partition holds tens of millions, interleaved by arrival. Chapter 7 again: shard key versus shard count. The key buys everything about A in one partition in seq order, read by one instance. It cannot buy a per-conversation position, which is why <code>seq</code> exists beside the offset.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Then is the offset redundant with seq?\"", a: "<p>Different coordinates. Offset: where a consumer is, how far behind, where to resume; per partition; not stable across a repartition. seq: order inside the conversation, unread arithmetic, the idempotency version on derived rows, gap detection (A#5 then A#7 means something was lost before the log). Neither derives from the other.</p>" },
          { q: "\"How many partitions, and what if you need more?\"", a: "<p>Take the max of three constraints: throughput per partition (tens of MB/s; our 30 MB/s needs two), consumer parallelism (search at ~5k/s per instance needs 12, the binding one), two years of headroom (64). Repartitioning is a migration: the key mapping changes, order breaks across the boundary, co-partitioned local state lands on the wrong instance. New topic, dual-publish, migrate groups one at a time. Over-provision up front; too many costs slow failovers and small batches.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "the key colocates; the count interleaves; offset says where you are in the log, seq where you are in the conversation." },
      { q: "\"Design the inbox consumer. Which join, where does state live, what happens on a crash mid-batch?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Stream–table join:</b> each event (keyed by <code>conv_id</code>) is looked up against a local participants copy fed by the Postgres CDC topic, co-partitioned by <code>conv_id</code> so each instance holds only its conversations' memberships; fan out to one inbox row per member. <b>State, two kinds, neither only in memory:</b> the participants copy, rebuilt from the compacted topic in minutes; the inbox rows, which are output in Cassandra keyed by <code>user_id</code> and versioned by <code>(conv_id, seq)</code>. <b>Crash mid-batch:</b> restart from the committed offset, re-read the events since, and apply-if-newer absorbs the duplicates; row count unchanged.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Why a local copy of participants instead of a Postgres lookup per event?\"", a: "<p>Sixty thousand lookups a second from this consumer alone; replay would enrich old messages with today's membership; Postgres would be on the critical path of every derived store. The rule: a local copy when this node's read rate dwarfs the table's total write rate, because every node receives every write regardless of how much it reads.</p>" },
          { q: "\"The consumer dies after writing output but before committing its offset.\"", a: "<p>It restarts from the committed offset and re-reads the events it already applied. Rows already carry those <code>(conv_id, seq)</code> versions, the conditional writes fail, nothing moves, a duplicates-skipped counter goes up. That is the normal path on every deploy, not an incident.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "stream–table against a co-partitioned participants copy, output versioned by (conv_id, seq), restart from the committed offset and let apply-if-newer absorb the replay." },
      { q: "\"Push notifications: one per message, never two, never zero. Walk the guarantee end to end.\"",
        a: "<p>Owner publishes (at-least-once from here). The notify consumer fans out per recipient: <code>(user, conv, seq)</code>. <b>Write the dedupe row first</b> (pending; skip if it exists). Send with a collapse key equal to the message identity (<code>conv_id:seq</code>, deterministic, never random, or no retry would ever match). Mark sent. Commit the offset. Crash points: before the row, replay proceeds (never zero); between row and send, zero unless a sweeper resends rows pending past a timeout; between send and mark, two unless the provider's collapse key replaces the first on the device; after mark, replay skips (never two). Honest guarantee: at-least-once to the provider, exactly-once on the screen. Skip the send if the recipient's watermark already passed <code>seq</code>.</p>",
        line: "dedupe row before the send, deterministic collapse key after; idempotency at the row plus idempotency at the device, not anything the broker did." },
      { q: "\"The search index is incremental now. Which join, what latency, and how do you reconcile against the nightly rebuild?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Stream–table</b>, same as the inbox, plus a tokenizer: event × participants copy → postings per user. <b>Latency: seconds</b>: tokenize, write a small new segment, visible after the refresh interval. What the incremental path accumulates: tiny segments (every query consults all), tombstones until a merge, drift. <b>Reconcile:</b> the nightly batch rebuilds from the snapshot at offset X into a fresh, compacted, tombstone-free set; a consumer replays from X onto it; flip the manifest; diff batch output against the live index for postings with version ≤ X. The batch does compaction, tombstone cleanup and proof; the stream does now.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"What does compaction mean for a search index?\"", a: "<p>Segment merge, not log compaction. Five messages with \"thursday\" arrived in five segments with one posting each; the merge writes one segment with one entry and a five-posting list, dropping tombstoned IDs. Log compaction keeps the latest value per key; a search merge keeps every posting.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "stream–table plus a tokenizer, seconds to visible, nightly rebuild to compact and prove; a search merge keeps every posting, log compaction keeps the latest." },
      { q: "\"Read receipts and typing indicators. Which belong on the durable log?\"",
        a: "<p>Test: does derived state depend on it, and is anyone wrong if it's lost? <b>Read receipts: on the log.</b> The unread count is \"seq above the watermark\"; the sender's \"seen\" is the other side's watermark; a replayed inbox has nothing else to rebuild it from. Cheap: one event per (user, conv) advance, merged with max, compactable, coalesced so a scroll is one event. <b>Typing: never.</b> Worth something for three seconds, nothing derived, high rate, corrected by the next one; in-memory pub/sub or straight through the connection fan-out. The same test sorts presence (ephemeral), mute settings (durable), delivered-to-device (on the log; the double tick depends on it).</p>",
        line: "on the log if something derived depends on it; read receipts yes, typing never." },
      { q: "\"Unread count per conversation. What's the window, what's the event time, and what about a phone that was offline for two days?\"",
        a: "<p>No time window at all. Unread is <code>max_seq − watermark</code>: a seq range, not a time range, so a three-day-old unread message still counts. Event time is seq, the one clock the conversation has. The offline phone's \"read up to 40\" arrives two days late while the laptop already advanced to 55; merge with max keeps 55 and the badge never jumps backward; on reconnect the server's view wins. Compaction loses nothing: latest watermark per key is exactly the state. The max is applied through the owner or as a conditional write (apply only if greater), never a plain overwrite; devices only propose.</p>",
        line: "unread is max_seq minus watermark in log position; the watermark merges with max, so late reads can only lower the count." },
      { q: "\"The inbox consumer group falls three hours behind. What do users see, how do you detect it, how do you recover?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Users see:</b> stale inboxes for every user whose conversations sit in the lagging partitions: old previews, wrong order, missing badges. Opening a conversation still works, because that view reads Cassandra directly (partition key <code>conv_id</code>, clustered by seq, <code>LIMIT 50</code>, one node, milliseconds): a lagging derived view degrades, it doesn't lose data. <b>Detect:</b> offset lag and time lag per partition, alerted, plus a freshness probe (a synthetic message timed until it appears). <b>Recover:</b> (1) catch up: scale instances to the partition count, make each faster, fix the cause (slow store, poison event dead-lettered, a bad deploy); (2) skip and backfill: reset offsets to the log end so users get current inboxes now, fill the three-hour hole with the chapter 11 batch over that offset range; version-by-offset makes the overlap safe. Prefer (2) when the gap is large.</p>",
        line: "the inbox degrades and the conversation view still works; watch lag, not previews; catch up if you can, skip and backfill if you can't." },
      { q: "\"Audit every place the app writes two stores. What's the fix for each?\"",
        a: "<p>Three questions per row: which single store does the app write, what carries it to the log, and is every downstream apply idempotent?</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Write</th><th>Single write + carrier</th><th>Consumers</th></tr><tr><td>Send</td><td>message + outbox in one Cassandra batch (same partition); relay</td><td>inbox, search, push, region 2, analytics</td></tr><tr><td>Edit / reaction / delete</td><td>same: events with <code>ref_seq</code> through the owner</td><td>inbox preview, search tombstone</td></tr><tr><td>Watermark</td><td>through the owner (applies the max), outbox</td><td>unread view, sender's seen</td></tr><tr><td>Participants</td><td>Postgres; outbox for intent, CDC on the outbox table</td><td>every owner's list, inbox and search copies, the new member's inbox row</td></tr><tr><td>Profile change, device tokens, feature flags</td><td>Postgres; CDC</td><td>previews, notify lookup copy, indexer</td></tr></table></div><p>Participant changes are their own topic, so a removal never queues behind messages; the membership event carries the seq at which it took effect.</p>",
        line: "one write per path, an outbox if the owner wrote it and CDC if Postgres did, and idempotent apply on every reader with no exceptions." },
      { q: "\"Messages per minute, live, per region, on a dashboard. Window, time, where does the number live?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Tumbling one-minute windows</b> (hopping if a smoother line is wanted), by <b>event time</b>, 30-second watermark, corrections allowed. Region is a field on the event (the conversation's home region), not a timezone. <b>Source: a metrics consumer group on the conversation-events topic</b>, like the other five; never Cassandra, never its write-ahead log. State checkpointed; output one row per (region, minute) into a real-time OLAP store or a Redis hash of recent hours; the dashboard reads that. The nightly batch recomputes the same counts from the lake and diffs them.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Why not processing time? It's simpler.\"", a: "<p>A twenty-minute consumer stall would show a dip then a spike nobody caused, and a phone reconnecting after two days would dump its queue into the wrong minute. The restart test: windowed by event time, replay the log and the graph is unchanged.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "tumbling by event time, region as a field, a consumer of the topic, landed in a store built for the dashboard." }
    ]},
    { id: "mechanisms", title: "Mechanisms, probed on the design", intro: "The chapter's concepts and tradeoffs, asked the way an interviewer asks them: when you choose it, where it breaks.", items: [
      { q: "\"Queue-style broker versus log-based broker. What does each forget, and when do you pick each?\"",
        a: "<p><b>A queue forgets the message once it's acked:</b> delivered to one consumer, deleted. Unacked messages wait and redeliver, breaking order. Durability is orthogonal (SQS is durable); what it forgets is history. <b>A log forgets nothing until retention or compaction:</b> reading deletes nothing; the broker stores one committed offset per consumer group, a cheap integer the consumer owns and can rewind. <b>Queue</b> when the unit is a task (transcode, email, resize): expensive per message, stateless interchangeable workers, no order, no replay. <b>Log</b> when it's an event stream: many readers at no cost per reader, replay to rebuild, order per key, millions per second.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Why does the log scale to many consumers at no extra cost?\"", a: "<p>A queue keeps state per message per consumer (delivered, acked, delete), and every ack is a write. A log keeps one integer per group; every consumer reads the same immutable bytes from the page cache, copied file-to-socket. Cost per consumer is bandwidth. Exception: a consumer far behind reads old segments from disk and slows the others.</p>" },
          { q: "\"Couldn't a queue just require all N subscribers to ack before deleting?\"", a: "<p>It exists (durable pub/sub subscriptions), and it's why logs won: per-message, per-subscriber ack state times N; the slowest subscriber pins everything; subscribers must register up front, so a new one gets nothing from yesterday. The log gives the same semantics with no per-message state: retain for a while, let readers track themselves.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "a queue hands out work and forgets it; a log records what happened and lets anyone read it, any time, in order." },
      { q: "\"Where does a log-based broker break?\"",
        a: "<p>Head-of-line blocking: one slow message stalls everything behind it in its partition, and a crashed consumer re-does it before moving on. Parallelism capped by partition count; extra workers idle. Repartitioning changes the key mapping, so order breaks across the boundary and co-partitioned local state is on the wrong node. Hot partitions from hot keys. Rebalances stop the group. No per-message delete: tombstone, retention, or crypto-shred. Retention is disk, and a lagging consumer hits it. Exactly-once is the consumer's job. Perfect for events, terrible for tasks.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"So add more partitions?\"", a: "<p>Only when the key spreads the load and the consumers need the parallelism. Ordering scope shrinks to the key; hot keys are untouched; per-partition overhead (files, replication streams, leader elections) makes failovers and rebalances slow in the thousands; producer batches shrink; the watermark is the minimum across more inputs, so more chances one is idle.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "perfect for events and terrible for tasks: ordered, replayable, fanned out, and one slow message stalls its partition." },
      { q: "\"Partitions and consumer groups: what's guaranteed about ordering, and what isn't?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Guaranteed:</b> total order within one partition; per-key order if you choose the key; one consumer per partition within a group, so that order survives into the consumer. <b>Not:</b> any order across partitions; order after a consumer processes in parallel threads or retries one event while moving on; parallelism beyond partition count; stability during a rebalance (reassignment replays from the last commit). Partitions are disjoint; each message lives in exactly one.</p>",
        line: "order is per partition, so put the thing that needs ordering in the key, and accept that parallelism is capped by partition count." },
      { q: "\"What is log compaction, what does it rebuild, and what does it cost?\"",
        a: "<p>Keep the latest value per key, drop older values; deletes become tombstones kept for a grace period. Bounded by distinct keys, so a ten-year changelog of a million users is a million records. Rebuilds anything that is current-state-per-key: a table, a cache, a stream processor's local copy. <b>Costs history:</b> it can rebuild what things are now, never what happened, so the message log is never compacted and the participants changelog can be. Never fully compacted at any instant, so a replaying consumer still sees several values per key and applies in order; a consumer down longer than the tombstone grace misses the delete.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Compaction or a snapshot?\"", a: "<p>Compaction for current-state-per-key, continuously, no snapshot step. Snapshot plus the raw log since for a consistent cutoff and history. Never compact between snapshots: that deletes exactly the intermediate values that let you rewind.</p>" },
          { q: "\"What is a tombstone, exactly?\"", a: "<p>A system primitive, not an application convention: deletion written as a new fact that outranks the old ones, because an append-only structure can't remove in place. Kafka: a key with a null value. Cassandra: a deletion timestamp replicas compare. LSM engines: a delete marker in the newest segment. It becomes your problem only when you build your own append-only thing.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "latest per key, tombstones for deletes, history gone; the storage for anything that is current-state-per-key." },
      { q: "\"Why is write-the-database-then-write-the-cache wrong, and what are the fixes?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Partial failure:</b> the database write succeeds, the app crashes before the cache write; the cache is stale with no record and nothing repairs it. <b>The race, no failure needed:</b> A writes 1 to the database, B writes 2, B writes 2 to the cache, A writes 1 to the cache; database says 2, cache says 1, forever. Atomicity across the two is two-phase commit with a cache as participant, and locks don't stop the race. <b>Fixes, one idea:</b> one writer of truth, everything else derived from its change stream. <b>CDC:</b> a consumer of the replication log updates the cache in commit order. <b>Outbox:</b> row plus event in one transaction; a relay publishes. Both are at-least-once to the consumer, so the cache write is versioned by log offset, apply-if-newer.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Is the CDC-fed cache one shared cache or a copy per node?\"", a: "<p>Either. Shared (Redis) updated by a small consumer group. Or each node is its own consumer group with an in-memory copy, bootstrapped from the compacted topic. Local wins when this node's read rate dwarfs the table's total write rate (<code>r ≫ W</code>): a config table at 10 writes a day, local; a counter at 10,000 writes a second, shared or no cache. Trap: fifty nodes as one group split the partitions and each sees a fiftieth.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "a crash leaves a hole and a race leaves the wrong order, both silent; one writer, derive the rest, apply idempotently." },
      { q: "\"Change data capture in one sentence. What's truth, what's derived, and how does the subscription work?\"",
        a: "<p>CDC reads the database's own replication log and publishes every committed row change as an ordered event stream, so other systems can follow the database without the app writing to them. <b>Truth:</b> the database. <b>Derived:</b> every consumer: cache, search, warehouse, inbox copy, cross-region copy. <b>Subscription is pull at two places:</b> one connector opens a replication connection and creates a slot, so the database keeps log from that position; the connector writes each change into a topic per table keyed by primary key; consumers subscribe at the broker by group name and pull at their own pace. Two hops because the slot is expensive for the database (it holds log for its slowest reader), so exactly one reader taps it.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Write-ahead log versus CDC?\"", a: "<p>The write-ahead log is the database's private physical record, written before data pages, for crash recovery and replica feeding, truncated once checkpointed. CDC is a process that reads it, decodes row changes, and publishes them externally. The write-ahead log is the thing; CDC is the tap. Not replica catch-up; that's chapter 6.</p>" },
          { q: "\"Where does CDC break?\"", a: "<p>The slot pins the log: a connector down for a week fills the primary's disk. It needs database cooperation (logical decoding, privileges, a connector per engine). Starting consistently needs a snapshot plus its exact log position. Schema changes arrive mid-stream. Events are mechanics (\"row inserted\"), not meaning (\"Bob joined\"). A ten-million-row migration is ten million events at once.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "the commit log made public; the database is truth and everything downstream is a fold of its changes." },
      { q: "\"Outbox versus CDC. When each, and how do the roles line up?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Outbox</b> when events should carry intent (\"Bob joined group 7\" from three table writes), the app owns the schema, and you publish only what the app chose; it's not an alternative to the queue but how the event gets into the queue safely. It breaks when a write path forgets to write the event (the migration script you find in six months), the relay is at-least-once, and the outbox table grows without cleanup. <b>CDC</b> is complete by construction, including migrations and DBA edits, and needs no app change. <b>Roles line up three for three:</b> where the change is recorded inside the transaction (write-ahead log, outbox table); the process that reads and publishes (connector, relay); the destination (the same log). The production combination is <b>CDC on the outbox table</b>: intent from the app, completeness from the log tap.</p>",
        line: "CDC gives every change as mechanics; the outbox gives the changes you chose, as intent; CDC on the outbox table gives both." },
      { q: "\"Event sourcing versus CDC. Who writes the events, and what do they mean?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Event sourcing:</b> the application writes the event deliberately as the record of intent (\"Alice added Bob\"); the log is truth, state is derived. <b>CDC:</b> the database emits the change as a side effect (\"row inserted in participants\"); the database is truth, the log is derived. Event-sourced history can be replayed through new logic for a different view; a CDC log replays only to the table it came from. The message log <i>is</i> event sourcing: <code>(conv_id, seq)</code> events, edits with <code>ref_seq</code>, the conversation view derived. The event log itself can't be compacted; it needs snapshots for replay speed and a derived view for reads.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Where does event sourcing break?\"", a: "<p>Reads need a derived view, which lags, so read-your-writes must be engineered. Validating a command needs current state, and two commands racing on a stale view both pass: optimistic concurrency on the stream version (the owner's seq). Event schemas are forever: a 2019 event must parse in 2030. The log grows without bound; snapshots bound replay time, not storage. Deletion versus immutability: crypto-shred. On a plain CRUD system it degrades into \"RowUpdated\" events, which is CDC with extra steps.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "intent written by the app versus mechanics emitted by the database; one can't be compacted, the other can." },
      { q: "\"Event time versus processing time. Give me one case where the wrong one changes the answer.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Windowed counts.</b> \"Messages per minute\" by processing time: a twenty-minute consumer stall shows a dip then a spike nobody caused, and a phone reconnecting after two days dumps its queue into the wrong minute. By event time, each message lands in the minute it was sent, and restarting the consumer changes nothing. Ordering within a conversation uses neither; the owner assigns seq. Processing time is right only for the pipeline: lag, throughput, timeouts and housekeeping inside the processor, disposable real-time tickers. Its cheapness, no event is ever \"late\" by it, is exactly why it's wrong for anything you'll look at twice.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"The phone crashed at 12:01 before sending; the message went at 12:04. Which time goes on it?\"", a: "<p>Three clocks: device event time 12:01, server receive 12:04, processing time whenever the analytics consumer saw it. Store the first two; display server receive (consistent across participants, independent of the sender's clock); order by seq. Analytics uses 12:01 or 12:04 consistently, never the third. Device clocks lying argues for server receive time as the event time, not for the processor's clock.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "event time for what the world did, processing time for what the pipeline did; if restarting the consumer changes the answer, you used the wrong one." },
      { q: "\"The four window types, and what a watermark does.\"",
        a: "<p>Tumbling (fixed, each event in one window), hopping (fixed, overlapping, for smoothing), sliding (every event's trailing interval), session (gap-based, no fixed length). <b>Watermark:</b> the processor's declaration \"no more events earlier than T,\" usually max event time seen minus allowed lateness, taken as the minimum across inputs. It lets a window close. Stragglers after it: drop, emit a correction, or keep state for an allowed-lateness grace. One dial: tight is fast with more stragglers; loose is late with fewer. Classic bug: an idle input freezes the minimum and every window stops closing, with no error.</p>",
        line: "a watermark is a guess that lets windows close; tune the dial, and watch for the idle input that freezes it." },
      { q: "\"The three joins. One example each, and the subtlety.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Stream–stream:</b> searches and clicks matched within an hour by session ID; state on both sides; \"no click\" emitted when the window closes. <b>Stream–table:</b> each event enriched from a local copy of the table kept current by the table's changelog, co-partitioned with the stream, never a remote lookup per event. <b>Table–table:</b> two changelogs maintained as one materialized view that updates when either side changes: the inbox copy, participants joined to messages. <b>Subtlety:</b> which version of the table? Old events get enriched with today's row unless the table is versioned by time; and two inputs means two lags, so an event can arrive before the row it references.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"When is a remote lookup per event right?\"", a: "<p>Only when the table is write-hot and events are rare: the <code>r ≫ W</code> rule inverted. Otherwise it's a round trip per event, the database serving the pipeline's load, and replay impossible because it looks up today's table.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "stream–stream in a window, stream–table against a local copy, table–table as a view; and ask which version of the table." },
      { q: "\"A stream-maintained materialized view versus computing at read time?\"",
        a: "<p>The view wins when reads are frequent and the query is heavy (a join or aggregate over many rows per request), when the read path must not touch the primary, or when the live query can't meet the latency target. The ratio: the view costs events × fan-out writes forever; live costs reads × query cost. Where the view breaks: lag (as current as its consumer, read-your-writes on top); drift (no memory of being correct, so periodic rebuild and diff); write amplification (one message in a 500-member group is 500 view updates); state must survive (checkpoints or a changelog topic); a logic change is a full replay; one view per query shape.</p>",
        line: "the view wins when reads are frequent and the query is heavy; a cheap query runs live, a rare heavy one isn't worth maintaining." },
      { q: "\"Checkpointed operator state, Flink style, or stateless workers with an external store?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Checkpointed:</b> high-throughput windowed aggregates and joins with lots of per-key state; state local, no network per event; state and offsets snapshotted together give exactly-once inside the job for free. Breaks on checkpoint and recovery time proportional to state, rescaling that redistributes key-partitioned state, state locked inside the job, and running a stateful cluster. <b>External store:</b> other services must read the state, workers must scale freely, heavy per-event work with small state. Breaks on a round trip per event, the store carrying the pipeline's load, state and offset not committed together (exactly-once is back on you), and two workers on one key racing unless partitioned by key. The honest split: Flink computes with local state; the result lands in Cassandra where the readers are.</p>",
        line: "checkpointed state for the computation, an external store for the result." },
      { q: "\"Exactly-once: what it means, and how you actually get it.\"",
        a: "<p>Delivery is at-least-once, always; exactly-once means each event's <i>effect</i> appears once despite redelivery. <b>Idempotent consumer</b>, the universal default into any store: set-not-increment when the event carries the value; otherwise version every row by event identity, apply-if-newer. A seen-before check on event identity is the construction of idempotence, not a cheat around it. <b>Transactional</b>, for the interior of a pipeline, Kafka to Kafka: output and offset in one commit; appends have nowhere to put a version, multi-output atomicity, a config flag. Built into Kafka since 2017 (commit markers, producer epochs fencing zombies). It ends the moment output leaves Kafka for Cassandra, an API, or an email. <b>Third construction:</b> don't process deliveries; recompute the derived store from the log.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Where does the idempotent consumer break?\"", a: "<p>A version column or dedupe table per output; per row, so a three-row effect can crash halfway; side effects can't be versioned (a dedupe row before the send, and a crash between them loses one); it needs a stable event identity. Locking is not an answer: the real duplicate is the same consumer an hour later after a crash.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "transactional where output and offset share a system; idempotent everywhere else; a real pipeline is transactions inside and idempotency at the sinks." },
      { q: "\"Replay the log to rebuild a derived store, or patch it in place?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Replay</b> when the wrong rows can't be enumerated (sporadic drift from delivery: duplicates, drops, out-of-order) or every row changes (schema, logic). <b>Patch</b> when they can: systematic drift from a code bug in a known offset window, or a backfill of one column. <b>Partial replay</b> for a consumer that fell behind: rewind to the offset and run forward. Replay needs retention reaching the point (else snapshot plus log since), a deterministic consumer, double capacity during the rebuild, and warms cold caches at the flip. Patch has no proof: new code on data you know is wrong, and the drift you didn't look for stays.</p>",
        line: "patch when you can enumerate the wrong rows; otherwise replay, and reconcile anyway for the drift you can't see." }
    ]},
    { id: "followups", title: "Interviewer follow-ups", intro: "The pushes that come after the stream is on the board.", items: [
      { q: "\"Why Kafka and not SQS here?\"",
        a: "<p>Shape, not reliability; SQS is durable. Fan-out: five groups need every message; SQS delivers to one and deletes. Ordering: standard SQS has none; FIFO orders per group at a few thousand a second against our 60k. Replay: rebuild the inbox, re-index, fix a consumer bug by re-running history. Retention as the record for derived stores. And SQS is right for the work queues in the same system: transcode, email, deletion tasks.</p>",
        line: "five readers, per-conversation order, and rebuild from history; SQS gives none, and we use it for the tasks anyway." },
      { q: "\"Explain exactly-once to a product manager in three sentences.\"",
        a: "<p>If anything in the system fails and retries, the user still sees each message, badge and notification exactly once. We do that by giving every event a name and refusing to apply the same name twice. So a failure can make something a few seconds late, but it can never make it show up twice or disappear. If pushed: under the hood things can be delivered twice; what we guarantee is that the second time has no effect.</p>",
        line: "name the promise, name the mechanism in plain words, name the one thing that can still happen: delay." },
      { q: "\"How do you re-process the last six months of events after fixing a bug in the consumer?\"",
        a: "<p>Retention decides whether replay is possible: six months on the log, tiered to object storage, or a snapshot plus log since; otherwise the lake. The participants topic is compacted, so a replay sees today's membership, not the membership as of each message. <b>The arithmetic:</b> six months at 60k/s is about a trillion events; a stream replay at ten times live speed is weeks. So: a few keys, replay just their partitions; one column across every row, the batch job over the lake overnight, then the stream consumer resumes from the batch's cutoff offset; full stream replay only when the store is small or the window short.</p>",
        line: "replay for short windows or few keys, the lake for long windows or every row, and the stream resumes from the rebuild's offset." },
      { q: "\"What's the difference between a Kafka topic and a database table?\"",
        a: "<p>Table: current state, one row per key, mutated in place, queryable by anything, identity by primary key, answers \"what is it now.\" Topic: history, one record per event in order, append only, read sequentially from an offset, identity by partition plus offset, lives until retention, answers \"what happened in what order.\" <b>The duality:</b> a table is a fold over its changelog (start empty, apply each change in order), and a changelog is the stream of changes to a table. Each derives from the other; a compacted topic is a table stored as a log.</p>",
        line: "a table is a fold over its changelog and a changelog is the stream of changes to a table; the log is the truth and every table is a fold of a prefix of it." },
      { q: "\"Your dashboard count is wrong for yesterday. Stream bug or batch bug, and how do you find out?\"",
        a: "<p>Both pipelines computed the same number from the same log, so <b>compute it a third way, directly from the log</b> (count events with event time in yesterday's range) and see which pipeline matches. Batch = log, stream differs: stragglers past the watermark, double-counting on restart without idempotent state, a processing-time midnight, lost window state. Stream = log, batch differs: a missed lake partition, a smeared snapshot cutoff, the wrong timezone. Both differ the same way: the source (relay gap, event-time stamping). All three agree: the dashboard's query or cache. Suspect the stream first; the batch exists to check it. A metrics consumer bug drops counts, never messages.</p>",
        line: "count it a third way from the log, see which pipeline matches, and suspect the stream first because the batch exists to check it." },
      { q: "\"What is backpressure, and how does each broker shape handle a slow consumer?\"",
        a: "<p>Lag measured two ways: offset lag (log end minus committed) and time lag (now minus last processed event time). A log absorbs a slow consumer on disk, for as long as retention lasts. A queue fills memory and then drops or blocks. A direct pipe blocks the sender, which in our system would be the owner's send path. That is why the broker sits between the owner and every consumer: the owner never waits for the slowest reader.</p>",
        line: "a log absorbs lag on disk, a queue in memory, a pipe in the sender; measure both offset lag and time lag." },
      { q: "\"Lambda or kappa?\"",
        a: "<p>Lambda: batch for correctness plus stream for freshness, merged at query time. Kappa: stream only, rebuild by replaying the log through the same code. Our design is neither doctrine: the online store serves, the stream patches it within seconds, the nightly batch rebuilds the search index and recomputes the dashboard counts and diffs the stream's work. The batch is the proof; the stream is now.</p>",
        line: "online serves, stream patches, batch rebuilds and checks; pick the mechanism per store, not the doctrine." }
    ]}
  ],
  takeaways: [
    "A log is a buffer that doesn't forget: durable, replicated, ordered per partition, read by anyone at their own offset. A queue hands out work and forgets it. Events go on a log; tasks go in a queue; a messaging system has both.",
    "One writer of truth, everything else derived from its change stream. The dual write fails on a crash (a silent hole) and on a race (a silent wrong order); the outbox or CDC removes both by making the event part of the one write.",
    "If the owner wrote it, it's an outbox; if Postgres wrote it, it's CDC; and the production shape is CDC on the outbox table. Never tap Cassandra's write-ahead log for a stream the owner already publishes.",
    "The owner decides, the broker remembers, the consumer derives. Every owner is a producer, not every producer is an owner, and collapsing any two roles makes the survivor do both jobs.",
    "Exactly-once is constructed, never delivered: an idempotent effect (naturally, or by a seen-before check on event identity, which is the construction of idempotence), or output and offset in one commit, or recompute from the log. Transactions inside a Kafka-to-Kafka pipeline, idempotency at every sink.",
    "Version every derived row by log position, (conv_id, seq) here, and apply only if newer. It makes replays, retries, batch backfills, and out-of-order events all harmless, and it's the same rule that stitched batch to stream in chapter 11.",
    "Event time for what the world did, processing time for what the pipeline did. If restarting the consumer changes the answer, you used the wrong one. A watermark is a guess with one dial, and an idle input freezes it.",
    "A stream-maintained view is current and can be wrong forever; batch output is stale and heals. Online serves it, the stream patches it, the batch rebuilds it and diffs the stream's work. Patch when you can enumerate the wrong rows; replay otherwise; reconcile anyway.",
    "A table is a fold over its changelog and a changelog is the stream of changes to a table. Compaction is where they meet. The stream–table join is a fold kept locally, co-partitioned with the stream, and it wins when reads per node dwarf total writes.",
    "Two coordinates, never one: the offset says where you are in the log and seq says where you are in the conversation. Partition count is sized by the slowest consumer's parallelism with two years of headroom, because repartitioning is a migration."
  ]
});
